To integrate low-resolution consistent disparity reconstruction and high-resolution fine-grained disparity recovery within a unified framework, we introduce a progressive cascade network: the low-resolution stage builds upon a 4D Geometry Encoding Volume (GEV) [ 27 , 7 , 28 ] that encodes non-local geometries and handles ill-posed regions for geometric consistency, while the high-resolution stage builds upon a 3D All-pairs Correlation Volume (ACV) [ 27 , 29 , 15 ] that establishes precise pixel correspondences for fine-grained details. Feature extraction. There are three encoders in feature extraction: (i) The feature encoder extracts matching features 𝐟 l , r \mathbf{f}^{l,r} from the stereo images pair 𝐈 l , r \mathbf{I}^{l,r} to construct stereo hypothesis volumes under epipolar constraints. (ii) The context encoder extracts context features 𝐜 l \mathbf{c}^{l} from the left image 𝐈 l \mathbf{I}^{l} to guide confidence propagation. (iii) The depth foundation model extracts monocular depth features 𝐝 l , r \mathbf{d}^{l,r} from the paired images 𝐈 l , r \mathbf{I}^{l,r} to provide geometrically consistent features. Furthermore, we integrate the monocular depth features into matching features to alleviate matching ambiguity in ill-posed regions, and into context features to mitigate domain biases from synthetic artifacts. Given the image pair 𝐈 l , r ∈ ℝ 3 × h × w \mathbf{I}^{l,r}\in\mathbb{R}^{{3}	imes h	imes w} , we construct the multi-scale matching pyramid of { 𝐟 s l , r ∈ ℝ c s × h / 2 s × w / 2 s } s = 1 4 \{\mathbf{f}^{l,r}_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{{c_{s}}	imes{
